          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Break the eggs into a bowl. Beat very gently with chopsticks. You want the yolks and whites combined but as few bubbles as possible. Do not whisk."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine the cooled dashi with the soy sauce, mirin, and sake. Stir to mix, then pour gradually into the beaten egg, stirring gently as you go."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Strain the egg mixture through a fine sieve into a jug. This removes any chalazae or undissolved egg white and gives a smoother result."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the tofu cubes between 4 heatproof cups or ramekins. Pour the egg mixture gently over the top, filling each cup to about 1cm below the rim."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cover each cup loosely with foil. Place in a steamer over water that is barely simmering. The steam should be gentle, not vigorous."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Steam for 15 to 18 minutes until the custard is just set. It should wobble slightly in the centre when the cup is tilted. A skewer inserted in the centre should come out clean."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the steamer. Top each cup with sliced spring onion and a small drop of sesame oil. The"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "umami"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "from the dashi comes through most clearly when served warm, not hot."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Surface is pitted with bubbles and looks curdled",
              "cause": "The steam was too hot, causing the egg proteins to set too quickly and unevenly.",
              "fix": "Keep the steam gentle throughout. Place a folded tea towel between the steamer basket and lid to prevent condensation dripping onto the custard and to lower the steaming temperature."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Custard is watery and has not set after 20 minutes",
              "cause": "The egg to dashi ratio is off, or the steam heat is too low.",
              "fix": "Recheck the ratio: it should be 1 egg to approximately 125ml of dashi. If the ratio is correct, increase the heat very slightly. The custard should set within 18 minutes under sufficient steam."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Custard tastes bland",
              "cause": "The dashi used was too weak or made with water alone.",
              "fix": "Use well-made dashi from kombu and bonito flakes, or good-quality instant dashi. Taste the seasoned dashi before adding the egg. It should taste pleasantly savoury on its own."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chawanmushi originated in Nagasaki during the Edo period, where it developed from Chinese steamed egg dishes introduced through trade. It spread across Japan as a restaurant starter and later became a home dish. In traditional Japanese kaiseki dining it appears early in the meal as a warm, delicate course between the soup and the main dishes. At home it is a quieter pleasure, eaten on cool evenings when something warming but light is needed."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}